::ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve the system y=x+2 and y=6. {
= x=4#Correct. Substitute y=6 into 6=x+2.
~ x=2#This uses the intercept only.
~ x=6#This is the y-value, not x.
~ x=8#This adds instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.4; MLA.A2.FN.5; MLA.A2.FN.6<br><br>Question:<br>How does y=(x-3)<sup>2</sup> transform y=x<sup>2</sup>? {
~ Left 3#The sign inside parentheses is interpreted oppositely.
= Right 3#Correct. x-3 shifts the graph right 3 units.
~ Up 3#There is no outside +3.
~ Down 3#There is no outside -3.
}

::ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8<br><br>Question:<br>If f(x)=x+1 and g(x)=4x, what is f(g(2))? {
~ 8#This stops after g(2).
~ 7#This applies functions in the wrong way.
= 9#Correct. g(2)=8 and f(8)=9.
~ 12#This multiplies after adding.
}

::ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.9; MLA.A2.FN.10<br><br>Question:<br>Find the inverse of f(x)=x-5. {
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x-5#This repeats the original.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=5x#This does not undo subtracting 5.
~ f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=1/(x-5)#Inverse notation is not reciprocal.
= f<sup>-1</sup>(x)=x+5#Correct. Add 5 to undo subtracting 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L08_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution is viable if a time model gives t=-4 and t=9? {
= t=9 only#Correct. Negative time is not viable.
~ t=-4 only#Negative time is not viable.
~ Both values#One value violates the context.
~ Neither value#The positive time is viable.
}

